Title: Programme Finance Officer for Africa
Type of contract: Employment
Contract duration: 24-month fixed term contract with possible extension
Work time: Full-time position (40 hours per week)
Reporting to: Regional Finance Coordinator for Africa
Location: Uganda, Sudan or Chad, with travel within the assigned portfolio where feasible
Start date: February 2027
Application deadline: 8 December 2026
Background
The Global Survivors Fund (GSF) was launched in October 2019 by Dr Denis Mukwege and Nadia Murad, Nobel Peace Prize laureates 2018. Its mission is to enhance access to reparations for survivors of conflict-related sexual violence around the globe, thus responding to a gap long identified by survivors. GSF acts to provide interim reparative measures in situations where states or other parties are unable or unwilling to meet their responsibilities. GSF advocates for duty bearers as well as the international community to develop reparations programmes. It also guides states and civil society by providing expertise and technical support for designing reparations programmes. GSF’s survivor-centric approach is the cornerstone of its work.
Role Summary
The Programme Finance Officer contributes to the effective financial management of GSF's programmes and partnerships across the assigned Africa portfolio. The role combines detailed financial expertise with practical support to Country Teams and partners, helping ensure that financial information is reliable, grants are well managed, risks are identified, and GSF resources are used effectively and responsibly. The postholder supports the Regional Finance Coordinator and exercises an appropriate level of autonomy according to the size, complexity and risk of the assigned portfolio.
Position Responsibilities
Major Programmes and Grants
For major programmes and grants, undertake specific finance responsibilities delegated by the Regional Finance Coordinator, within an agreed scope, timeline and review framework.
These responsibilities could include:

• Reviewing partner financial reports, expenditure and supporting documentation and following up variances, compliance issues and outstanding information;

• Preparing or updating budgets, forecasts, grant-monitoring information, reconciliations, schedules and financial analysis;

• Contributing to partner financial assessments, risk reviews and follow-up of mitigation or capacity-strengthening actions;

• Supporting donor financial reporting, audits, expenditure verifications and grant close-out;

• Providing practical partner coaching on grant delivery, budget monitoring, reporting, documentation and agreed GSF tools;

• Maintaining accurate financial information, grant records and action tracking.

Small Grants and Smaller Country Programmes

• Act as the day-to-day Finance Partner to Country Coordinators for the assigned portfolio, providing financial advice and constructive challenge;

• Manage routine financial planning, monitoring, partner reporting, expenditure review, cash requests, compliance follow-up and grant close-out;

• Apply proportionate controls and partner support according to grant value, risk, partner maturity and operating context;

• Maintain appropriate financial records and escalate material risks, exceptions, suspected misuse, significant control concerns and decisions outside delegated authority.

Coordination and Accountability

• Agree priorities, delegated responsibilities, timelines and review arrangements with the Regional Finance Coordinator;

• Coordinate with Programme, Country and MEL colleagues so that financial follow-up reflects programme progress and partner performance;

• Contribute to portfolio reviews, programme visits, audits and Finance-team learning when relevant to assigned responsibilities;

• Undertake other programme-finance responsibilities consistent with the role and assigned portfolio.

